          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Commander of the Continental Army</strong><br>\nIn 1775, the Second Continental Congress appointed Washington as commander-in-chief of the Continental Army. He led the colonial forces during the Revolutionary War, facing significant challenges including limited resources and training. Despite these hardships, Washington's persistent strategy and leadership culminated in the surrender of British General Cornwallis in 1781.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Constitutional Convention</strong><br>\nWashington presided over the Constitutional Convention in 1787, which was responsible for drafting the United States Constitution. His reputation for fair play and his commitment to American republicanism made him a unifying figure. The Constitution's ratification led to a stronger federal government, laying the groundwork for the new nation's political system.</p>\n\n<p><strong>First Presidential Election</strong><br>\nWashington was unanimously elected as the first President of the United States in 1789. His election set many protocols for the federal government's role and the presidency. As president, Washington worked to establish a strong, financially stable national government while remaining non-partisan and avoiding the formation of political parties.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
