          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Domestic Policies</strong><br>\nDuring his presidency, Washington set the precedent for how a president should govern. He established the first United States Cabinet and the executive branch's operations. His administration implemented the Judiciary Act of 1789 and the creation of a national bank, playing a crucial role in shaping the nation's economic policy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Foreign Policy and Neutrality</strong><br>\nWashington's foreign policy was characterized by neutrality, avoiding entanglement in European conflicts. He issued the Neutrality Proclamation in 1793, which kept the United States neutral during the war between Britain and France. This policy paved the way for peaceful trade and allowed the young nation to strengthen economically.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Washington's Legacy</strong><br>\nWashington declined a third term in office, setting the two-term precedent for future presidents. He retired to his Mount Vernon estate, where he passed away on December 14, 1799. His farewell address and acts in office established many standards for the presidency and governance, and he remains a pivotal figure in American history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
